# CAT2a: If an active alarm of equal or higher priority represents the same
# biological process of an incoming alarm, then the incoming alarm
# concerning that process can be silenced.
#
# ?alarm is pre-bound by assess.py (the arriving alarm). Reuses
# EVALUATION/CQs/temporal/CQ10_coinciding_equivalence.rq's "clinical" branch
# (two alarms reaching the same PhysiologicalProcess via their independent
# approximates/isPropertyOf chains — mda:situational-tagged, so already
# present in the reasoned situational graph assess.py builds, no extra
# reasoning pass needed here), restricted to an ALREADY-ACTIVE alarm (not
# merely coinciding in one tick) and gated by mda:priorityRank — see
# FRAMEWORK/VOCABULARY/priority_rank.ttl, unioned into the query graph by
# assess.py, not part of op_knowledge.py's own reasoning_static.
#
# ?active being present in the query graph at all already means it is
# active-or-persisting at ?alarm's arrival instant (assess.py builds the
# graph from Timeline.situation_at/background_at at that exact timestamp);
# this query does not re-derive that, only excludes ?alarm matching itself.
#
# BOTH ?alarm and ?active are required to be mda:hasCategory
# alarmcat:Physiological — found necessary, not just tidy, while validating
# against DATA/CAT_evaluation/cat1a_pos: a Technical-category alarm (e.g.
# "PHILIPSMONITOR - Storing in SpO2", whose own archetype has NO Metric node
# at all — see kg_generated.ttl) was matching as ?active anyway, because
# Sensor/Signal particulars are minted per (device_id, concept) — correctly,
# it IS the same physical sensor — so a COINCIDING alarm's own
# Metric/approximates chain, hung off that same shared Signal node, is
# reachable by walking from the Technical alarm's OWN triggeredBy path too.
# The framework has no edge tying a Metric to the specific alarm that
# reported it (Metric is Stateful/condition-scoped, but shared across every
# alarm currently active on that pathway) — so without this guard the query
# credits a technical fault with clinical content it never asserted, purely
# because it happens to share a sensor with a physiological alarm. A
# Technical alarm has no claim to "representing a biological process" in
# the first place, so the guard is correct on its own terms, not only a
# workaround.

PREFIX mda: <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX alarmcat: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/alarm-category/>

SELECT ?active ?process WHERE {
  ?alarm mda:hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological .
  ?alarm mda:hasMessage/mda:triggeredBy/mda:hasSensor/
         mda:sensorProducesSignal/mda:analyzedBy/mda:producesMetric/
         mda:approximates/mda:isPropertyOf ?process .
  ?alarm mda:hasPriority ?incomingPrio .
  ?incomingPrio mda:priorityRank ?incomingRank .

  ?active mda:hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological .
  ?active mda:hasMessage/mda:triggeredBy/mda:hasSensor/
          mda:sensorProducesSignal/mda:analyzedBy/mda:producesMetric/
          mda:approximates/mda:isPropertyOf ?process .
  ?active mda:hasPriority ?activePrio .
  ?activePrio mda:priorityRank ?activeRank .

  FILTER(?active != ?alarm)
  FILTER(?activeRank >= ?incomingRank)
}
